# A binding that says how a handle is GIVEN BACK must say how each one is
# HANDED OVER (panel 148 R5, M-marked-acquisition; widened by panel 149).
#
# **The rule.** Where any `extern` function consumes a handle type `T`, every
# `extern` function handing a `T` back — as a result or through an `@`
# out-parameter — says which it is: `acquires <releaser>` when the program owes
# a release for it, `borrows` when the library keeps it. Unmarked is an error
# naming both lines.
#
# **Why it is keyed on the TYPE and not on the group, which is where panel 148's
# compiler seat and its critic disagreed.** That seat priced a group-keyed rule
# at ~90 lines over a key the language does not define: `parse/group.hero` says
# *"the group is flattened here and only here… nothing after the parser knows
# the word"*, and this compiler's own source holds two `extern "hero_os.h"`
# groups and two `extern "stdlib.h"` ones, so a header-text key merges what
# should stay apart and a head-line key loses a binding split across modules —
# which §4.19 FORCES, since an `extern` is not callable across a module
# boundary. The critic found the answer already shipped: `check/decls.hero`'s
# `one_tag_one_type` is 17 lines and program-wide, and **one tag is one handle
# type across the whole program**. So the question needs no group identity, no
# span reconstruction and no new kind of table.
#
# **What this rule is NOT.** It does not ask whether a release happens on every
# path — that is a flow question and `check/leasing.hero:29` and
# `check/consuming.hero:22` both state the checker has no flow analysis. The
# counter in `runtime/parts/alloc.c` answers that one at exit. This rule asks
# only whether the BINDING is finished, which is a fact about declarations.
#
# **And it is why `borrows` exists.** Without a word for the borrow this rule
# would refuse a correct binding — `sqlite3_next_stmt` hands back a statement
# SQLite keeps — and the only escape would be to claim `acquires`, which the
# compiler would then enforce by making the program give back what was never
# its own. That is the objection that killed the inferred form at the same
# sitting, and adopting half of the answer to it brought it back.
#
# ## What panel 149 changed, 2026-09-14, and why both halves were needed
#
# **A handle is what a type REACHES, not what it IS**, through group-record
# fields and fixed-array elements, to any depth (`check/reaches.hero`). As
# shipped this rule asked whether a declaration's type WAS a handle, so
# `pair_make(n: i64) -> Pair` with a `Slot` in a field carried no mark, compiled
# in silence, and aborted at exit 134. That was defect 033.
#
# **And it is asked of the CONSUMER side too, which is the half the sitting
# nearly missed.** `consumed_types` asked at one level, so
# `UnloadFont(font: Font consumes)` — a record with fields — left the map empty
# and the rule returned at its first line. Measured at the sitting: a
# producer-only widening **stays silent on raylib's `Font`, the only real
# shipped-library case, while erroring on the contrived one.** The sitting's
# completeness critic found that, and it is the reason the walk is called twice
# here rather than once.
#
# **The releaser a mark names is checked.** Until 2026-09-14 the word was never
# read: `acquires sqlite3_notafunction` and `acquires sqlite3_finalize` on a
# `Db` both checked and built at exit 0, measured. design.md Part 6 already
# rules on that shape — *"A tag nobody reads is a comment that looks like a
# guarantee, which is the one thing an FFI must never carry."* Widening a
# mandatory word without reading it multiplies the places it can lie.
#
# **What panel 149 did NOT do, so nobody re-proposes it as new.** It refused to
# refuse a record reaching more than one handle. All three candidates fell to
# measurements taken at the sitting: raylib's `Font` reaches two handles,
# carries one `acquires UnloadFont`, and exits 0, so a refusal would refuse a
# binding that works; moving the releaser onto the handle type is panel 147's
# Route A, refused there on its own axis. **One mark is one obligation on the
# whole value**, which is what C ships — `freeaddrinfo` is one call for a chain,
# `UnloadModel` one for four arrays.

use ast
use source
use token
use diag
use check/state
use check/reaches

# Every handle type some `extern` function takes back. The map is keyed on the
# handle's DECLARATION, which `one_tag_one_type` makes a program-wide identity.
#
# It asks what the parameter's type REACHES, so a releaser taking the composite
# — `UnloadFont(font: Font consumes)` — registers the handles inside it. Asking
# whether the parameter IS a handle left this map empty for every real binding
# that frees a struct, and an empty map makes the whole rule return at its first
# line (panel 149's critic).
function consumed_types(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl]) -> {i64: bool}
    taken: {i64: bool} @ {}

    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern
                    for p in fd.value.params
                        if p.consumes
                            behind = handle_behind(c, s, decls, node: p.ty)

                            if !behind.is_err()
                                taken[behind.must().decl] @ true
            .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => taken @ taken

    return taken

# What the TYPE NODE a declaration wrote reaches, if it reaches a handle.
#
# It goes through `written_types` for `ffi_sweep`'s reason: the node is what the
# author wrote and the interned id is what the checker knows, and only the
# second can be asked what KIND of type it is.
function handle_behind(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], node: i64) -> reaches.Reached?
    written = c.out.written_types[node]

    if written.is_err()
        return fail(code: "none", msg: "this node has no written type")

    return reaches.reaches_handle(c, s, decls, ty: written.must())

# One function that takes this handle type back, as its NAME SPAN: the
# diagnostic names both lines, and this is the second one.
#
# **IT RETURNED A BARE NAME UNTIL 2026-09-15, AND THAT WAS DEFECT 041.** This
# rule is program-wide — `one_tag_one_type` makes a handle's declaration a
# program-wide identity, and `checker.hero` checks one resolved PROGRAM — so the
# refusal lands in whatever file declares the producer, which may be a file whose
# author never wrote the `consumes` that armed it and which imports nothing.
# Measured in two files: the error landed in `slots.hero` because `main.hero`
# declared `slot_close`, naming `slot_close` and never saying where it was.
#
# design.md §4.17 asks a diagnostic to carry everything needed without opening
# another file, and here a reader could not learn WHICH other file. **The answer
# already shipped**: `source.elsewhere` says `line 12` when both ends are in one
# file and `geom.hero:12` when they are not, which is exactly the rule §4.17
# wants — six other diagnostics call it and this one did not.
function taker_of(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], handle: i64) -> token.Span?
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern
                    for p in fd.value.params
                        if p.consumes
                            behind = handle_behind(c, s, decls, node: p.ty)

                            if !behind.is_err() && behind.must().decl == handle
                                return ok(decl.name)
            .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

    return fail(code: "absent", msg: "no extern of this program takes one back")

# The rule itself.
function bindings_say_which(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    taken = consumed_types(c, s, decls)

    if taken.len() == 0
        return

    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern
                    result_handle = handle_behind(c, s, decls, node: fd.value.result)

                    if !result_handle.is_err() && !taken[result_handle.must().decl].is_err()
                        if fd.value.acquires_result.is_err() && !fd.value.borrows_result
                            state.push_diagnostic(
                                @c,
                                refusal_for(c, s, decls, handle: result_handle.must().decl, producer: decl.name, route: result_handle.must().path, at: decl.name)
                            )
                        else
                            releaser_reads(
                                @c,
                                s,
                                decls,
                                mark: fd.value.acquires_result,
                                reached: result_handle.must()
                            )

                    for p in fd.value.params
                        if p.mutable
                            behind = handle_behind(c, s, decls, node: p.ty)

                            if !behind.is_err() && !taken[behind.must().decl].is_err()
                                if p.acquires.is_err() && !p.borrows
                                    state.push_diagnostic(
                                        @c,
                                        refusal_for(c, s, decls, handle: behind.must().decl, producer: decl.name, route: behind.must().path, at: p.name)
                                    )
                                else
                                    releaser_reads(@c, s, decls, mark: p.acquires, reached: behind.must())
            .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

# The word a mark names has to name something, and the something has to END the
# life the mark BEGINS. Two facts about declarations, neither of them a flow
# question: the name resolves to an `extern` of this module, and that `extern`
# consumes a parameter reaching the same handle.
#
# `borrows` gets no such check and needs none: it names no function, which is
# the whole reason it is a bare word.
function releaser_reads(@c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], mark: token.Span?, reached: reaches.Reached)
    if mark.is_err()
        return
    named = source.span_text(s, mark.must())

    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern && source.span_text(s, decl.name) == named
                    for p in fd.value.params
                        if p.consumes
                            behind = handle_behind(c, s, decls, node: p.ty)

                            if !behind.is_err() && behind.must().decl == reached.decl
                                return
            .constant_decl | .record_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

    state.push_diagnostic(
        @c,
        unread_releaser(named: named, handle: source.span_text(s, decls[reached.decl].name), span: mark.must())
    )

# The refusal. It names the handle, the call that takes one back, and the two
# words that answer it — because §4.17 asks that an error carry everything
# needed to fix the program without opening another file. Where the handle is
# not the result's own type it names the ROUTE to it, because the line the caret
# sits on shows no handle at all: `LoadFont(fileName: cstr) -> Font` is a
# perfectly ordinary signature until somebody opens `record Font`.
# The refusal, built from the declarations rather than from strings, so the two
# call sites cannot drift on what they pass. `where_taken` is `source.elsewhere`'s
# answer: silent when the taker is on screen, `slots.hero:14` when it is not.
function refusal_for(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], handle: i64, producer: token.Span, route: str, at: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    taker = taker_of(c, s, decls, handle: handle)
    taker_name: str @ ""
    where_taken: str @ ""

    if !taker.is_err()
        taker_name @ source.span_text(s, taker.must())
        there = source.elsewhere(s, here: at.start, there: taker.must().start)

        if source.file_of(s, at.start) != source.file_of(s, taker.must().start)
            where_taken @ ", at " + there

    return unmarked(
        producer: source.span_text(s, producer),
        taker: taker_name,
        where_taken: where_taken,
        handle: source.span_text(s, decls[handle].name),
        route: route,
        span: at
    )

function unmarked(producer: str, taker: str, where_taken: str, handle: str, route: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    where: str @ "hands back a `" + handle + "`"

    if route != ""
        where @ "hands back a `" + handle + "` inside `" + route + "`"
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "unmarked_handle_producer",
        message: "`" + producer + "` " + where + " and does not say whether the program owes it — `" + taker + "` takes one back" + where_taken + ", so this binding has said how a `" + handle + "` ends and not how it begins",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "write `acquires " + taker + "` if the call gives the handle away and the program must return it, or `borrows` if the library keeps it and the program owes nothing. No C header states which, so the binding author states it: `sqlite3_prepare_v2` gives a statement and `sqlite3_next_stmt` lends one, and their C signatures are the same shape"
    )

    if route != ""
        d.notes @ d.notes.push(
            "one mark answers for the whole value, however many handles it carries: C frees a composite with one call, and `" + route + "` is only where the first one sits"
        )
    return d

# The second refusal, and it exists because a mandatory word nobody reads is
# worse than no word: design.md Part 6 calls it *a comment that looks like a
# guarantee*.
function unread_releaser(named: str, handle: str, span: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @ diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "unread_releaser",
        message: "`" + named + "` does not end a `" + handle + "`'s life: no `extern` of this module declares it taking one back with `consumes`",
        span: span
    )
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "`acquires` names the call that ENDS what this one begins, so the name has to be an `extern` in this module with a `" + handle + "` parameter marked `consumes`. Declare it, or write `borrows` if the library keeps the handle and the program owes nothing"
    )
    return d
